"""A source-linked, progressively enhanced global history of mind."""
import json, html
E=html.escape
ERAS=['Before writing','Ancient accounts','500–1500','1500–1800','1800–1950','1950–present']
THEMES={
'life':('Life & animation','The capacities that distinguish a living being.'),
'self':('Soul & self','An enduring subject, or an identity to explain.'),
'heart':('Heart & feeling','Thought, emotion and moral character together.'),
'process':('Change & process','Mental life as events, habits and transformations.'),
'awareness':('Awareness & knowing','Experience and the conditions of knowledge.'),
'body':('Body & brain','Perception, action and bodily organization.'),
'relations':('People & worlds','Language, community, power and personhood.'),
'practice':('Cultivation & skill','Understanding through disciplined transformation.'),
'information':('Learning & information','Adaptation, computation and intelligent performance.')}
def render(root):
 data=json.loads((root/'history.json').read_text())
 def options(values):return ''.join(f'<option>{E(v)}</option>' for v in sorted(set(values)))
 motifs=''.join(f'<button type="button" disabled data-theme="{k}" aria-pressed="false"><span class="theme-index">{i:02d}</span><strong>{E(t)}</strong><span>{E(d)}</span><small>{sum(k in x["themes"] for x in data)} accounts <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></small></button>' for i,(k,(t,d)) in enumerate(THEMES.items(),1))
 eras=''.join(f'<button type="button" disabled data-era="{E(era)}" aria-pressed="false"><strong>{E(era)}</strong><span>{sum(x["era"]==era for x in data)} {'account' if sum(x["era"]==era for x in data)==1 else 'accounts'}</span></button>' for era in ERAS)
 entries=[]
 for i,d in enumerate(data,1):
  sources=''.join(f'<li><a href="{E(s["url"])}">{E(s["label"])}</a><span>{E(s["review"])}.</span></li>' for s in d['sources'])
  metadata=' · '.join([d['region'],d['tradition']])
  attrs=' '.join(f'data-{k}="{E(v)}"' for k,v in [('era',d['era']),('region',d['region']),('tradition',d['tradition']),('disciplines','|'.join(d['disciplines'])),('themes','|'.join(d['themes']))])
  entries.append(f'''<article class="history-entry" id="{d['id']}" {attrs}><div class="history-date"><span class="entry-number">{i:02d}</span><p>{E(d['date'])}</p><span>{E(d['era'])}</span></div><div class="history-account"><p class="history-meta">{E(metadata)}</p><h3><a href="#{d['id']}">{E(d['title'])}</a></h3><p class="history-answer">{E(d['answer'])}</p><p class="history-disciplines">{E(' / '.join(d['disciplines']))}</p><details><summary>Read the account &amp; sources</summary><div class="history-depth"><p>{E(d['depth'])}</p><p class="history-work">{E(d['work'])}</p><p class="history-limit"><strong>Context &amp; limits.</strong> {E(d['limit'])}</p><ul class="history-sources">{sources}</ul><p class="history-checked">Editorial check: 10 October 2026 · <a href="#{d['id']}">Link to this account</a></p></div></details></div></article>''')
 return f'''<link rel="stylesheet" href="history.css"><script src="history.js" defer></script>
<section class="history-opening"><p class="history-kicker">Foundations / A global history in development</p><h1>Humanity’s search for mind.</h1><p class="standfirst">How have humans tried to answer the question—what is mind?</p><p>Through souls and selves, hearts and brains, dreams and experiments. Explore accounts across cultures, religions and disciplines, from evidence before writing to contemporary research.</p><nav class="history-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#synthesis">The overview</a><a href="#history-explore">Explore {len(data)} accounts</a><a href="#coverage">Coverage &amp; gaps</a><a href="mind-words.html">Words, voices &amp; bodies</a></nav></section>
<section id="synthesis" class="history-synthesis"><div><p class="history-kicker">The history in brief</p><h2>Many answers. Different ways of knowing.</h2><p>“Mind” is our English starting point. The accounts below sometimes concern life, soul, self, attention or personhood instead. Their differences are part of the history.</p></div><ol><li><strong>Explain experience.</strong><span>Dreaming, remembering, suffering and skilled action can lead to very different accounts of a person.</span></li><li><strong>Choose a way to know.</strong><span>Argument, revelation, introspection, cultivation, art and experiment make different kinds of evidence available.</span></li><li><strong>Live with the answer.</strong><span>Accounts of mind can shape education, healing, responsibility and relations with other beings.</span></li></ol><p class="synthesis-note">An editorial synthesis of the linked accounts. Traditions overlap, disagree internally and continue into the present; this is not a single progression from religion to science.</p></section>
<section class="history-themes" aria-labelledby="theme-heading"><div class="history-section-heading"><h2 id="theme-heading">Recurring approaches</h2><p>Follow an idea across time. These are editorial comparisons, not fixed categories of peoples.</p></div><div class="theme-grid" id="theme-buttons">{motifs}</div></section>
<section id="history-explore"><div class="history-section-heading"><h2>Explore the history</h2><p>Grouped by broad period. Dates refer to particular works, objects or documented accounts—not the beginning or end of a culture. BCE and CE use the common calendar era.</p></div><div class="era-rail" role="group" aria-label="Choose a period">{eras}</div><form class="history-filters" id="history-filters" hidden><label>Search accounts<input type="search" id="history-search" placeholder="Dreams, Jikji, soul, music…"></label><label>Region<select id="history-region"><option value="">All regions</option>{options(d['region'] for d in data)}</select></label><label>Tradition or approach<select id="history-tradition"><option value="">All traditions</option>{options(d['tradition'] for d in data)}</select></label><label>Discipline<select id="history-discipline"><option value="">All disciplines</option>{options(v for d in data for v in d['disciplines'])}</select></label><button type="button" id="history-reset">Reset all filters</button></form><div class="history-results"><p id="history-count" role="status" aria-live="polite">{len(data)} accounts</p><a href="history.json" download>Download the research index</a></div><p id="history-empty" hidden>No accounts match this combination. Reset the filters to see the full collection.</p><div id="history-entries">{''.join(entries)}</div></section>
<section class="history-connections"><h2>Ideas travel through people and materials.</h2><div><article><h3>Translation changes the vocabulary</h3><p>The Arabic philosophical tradition engaged Greek works; <a href="#islamic-philosophy">its thinkers also developed their own accounts</a>. Translation is an intellectual event, not a transparent transfer of equivalent words.</p></article><article><h3>Mind and print meet in Korea</h3><p><a href="#jikji">Jikji’s surviving 1377 metal-type volume</a> makes the material history of Buddhist teachings visible. The history of printed inquiry begins well before a European-only account would suggest.</p></article><article><h3>Contemporary research joins older disputes</h3><p><a href="#solms">Feeling</a>, <a href="#levin">living organization</a> and <a href="#berg">artificial systems</a> bring new methods to enduring disagreements. Similar vocabulary does not establish that an ancient text predicted a modern finding.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="coverage" class="history-coverage"><h2>Global scope. Visible limits.</h2><p>This edition contains {len(data)} selected accounts. It spans evidence before writing, ancient texts, later religious and philosophical debates, living traditions, arts and modern research. It does not yet represent every culture, religion or discipline. A missing entry marks a gap in this collection, not an absence of thought.</p><div class="coverage-grid"><div><h3>What the sources can establish</h3><p>Primary texts show particular arguments. Museum records connect objects to documented contexts. Scholarly overviews reconstruct debates. Experiments test specific claims. Each entry identifies its source and review depth; an abstract-level check is not a full-paper review.</p><p>Older English translations remain accessible starting points, with vocabulary and interpretations that deserve comparison against current scholarship and original languages.</p></div><div><h3>Priority gaps</h3><p>Australian Aboriginal traditions; further African, Pacific and Indigenous American accounts; Southeast and Central Asia; Tibetan traditions; Zoroastrian and Bahá’í sources; additional Jewish, Christian, Hindu and Islamic schools; women’s intellectual histories; disability and neurodiversity; linguistics, psychiatry, dance and sport.</p><p>These require named communities, works and specialist or community-led sources. We will not fill the gaps with invented pan-cultural summaries.</p></div></div><p class="history-note">Broad regional labels are navigation aids, not civilizational boundaries. Living traditions appear at the dates of the documented accounts used here. The nine recurring approaches are comparisons made by this project, not an indigenous classification or a ranking.</p><a href="participate.html">Contribute a source or correction</a> · <a href="syllabus.html">Read the model syllabus</a></section>'''
